Answer:

1. What is the business problem?
Write 4-6 sentences.
  SKMCH&RC treats thousands of cancer patients across many cancer types, but treatment success
  ranges from 85% in breast cancer down to 52% in liver cancer (HCC). The hospital needs a way to identify,
  at diagnosis, which patients are at greater risk of poor treatment outcomes so the care teams can intervene
  earlier or allocate more intensive resources. Currently, the risk of poor outcomes is likely estimated mainly
  through clinical judgment and general statistics by cancer type, rather than a personalized, data-driven risk score
  for each patient. An AI model could consider factors like cancer type, stage at diagnosis, and patient age to predict
  the likelihood that treatment will work or fail in each case.


2. Why is this problem important to the organization?
Write 4-6 sentences.
The hospital has a mix of donated and paid procedures, so resources are limited and need to be used where they will
do the most good. This way, the hospital can more accurately predict which patients are at higher risk and prioritize
scarce chemo/radiation sessions and surgical planning for those who need more monitoring. It also helps counsel patients
and families more accurately about expected outcomes. For example, patients with oral cavity cancers usually present at
Stage II-III due to limited access to screening in KPK. Early risk flagging can significantly improve survival rates.
This directly supports the hospital’s mission to maximize patient outcomes within resource constraints.


